  "retained_diagnostics": "The first new unit fault case unmapped the page containing its kernel exception stack as well as the intended operand. Moving the fixture exception stack to a separate mapped page fixed observation of the original operand #PF. The original log is retained; production event delivery was unchanged.",
  "source_reference": {
    "title": "Intel SDM Volume 2, revision 093: BSWAP, XLAT and LEAVE",
    "url": "https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html",
    "local_text": "/mnt/data/neverd-paged-scheduling-20261101/intel-sdm-vol2-093.txt"
  },
  "boundaries": [
    "The existing scalar binding is required. BSWAP assumes instruction availability on the bound CPU; its undefined word form fails clearly instead of inventing a value. XLAT captures unsigned AL, truncates the sum to address width before segment-base addition and reads exactly one byte.",
    "LEAVE selects the frame through SS.B, independently pops its operand width, preserves partial-register high bits and stages ESP/EBP until completion. Faulting memory access restarts with the original frame state. These operations introduce no undefined-flags profile.",
    "LA57 is unavailable on the recorded CPU. Unlisted legacy scalars, the general i386 exporter, real/VM86, tasks/call gates, debug producers, software SMM/enclave/AEX/EPCM and complete CET/FRED/event domains are not certified.",
    "This change qualifies the original-byte software executor. It does not add a new native LLVM/NeverC API for XLAT or LEAVE, enable a general i386 frontend, or close global completion and final SDK release/pin gates."
  ],
